From a58660ee1fdfabae5cf8584eb33d70956a85aa5e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 10:32:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 140/274] DeepSeek-V4.1 Engram: read the rows the host can hash
 before the step

A disk Engram table's rows were read only after the GPU hashed the step's
tokens at the start of the step, so the first Engram layer waited for its
table: 4-36 ms per prefill chunk, 53-119 ms per 16K prefill on TP3.

The runner prepares a step a step ahead of the GPU, and a prefill chunk's
token ids are on the host then. The model state keeps each request's
prefill tokens and hashes its chunk rows on the host (host_hash.py, the
TileLang hash's arithmetic: wrapping multiplies, XOR, floor remainder)
and passes them with the step as early IDs; rows of tokens the GPU
samples stay UNKNOWN and come from the GPU's hash. One reader serves both:
it reads the early rows as soon as the GPU has gathered the previous step
from the same buffers (the gather now ends with a one-thread release of
CONSUMED_SEQ), waits for the GPU's IDs only if a lookup is unknown, reads
those, and publishes the step once. A step of prompt rows is ready before
the GPU reaches it; a decode step is unchanged; a mixed step reads its
prompt rows early and its decode rows late.

Adaptive verification gives verifying requests GPU-chosen row counts but
keeps them at the front of the batch with the host's total, so later
requests start at the same row on both sides; the host refuses a step
where a verifying request follows a prefill chunk.
SPARK3_ENGRAM_CHECK_EARLY_IDS=1 (debug, off in serving) compares every
early ID with the GPU's and names the first difference.

